      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62025,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:07:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62026,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most patients present with symptoms related to a pelvic mass. Hormonal manifestations are usually absent {18971779}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:07:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62027,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients have ranged in age from 23 to 71&nbsp;years (mean: 45&nbsp;years). The tumour may rarely occur as an extracolonic manifestation of familial adenomatous polyposis {25820106; 29076875; 27015438}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:07:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62028,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours may be associated with familial adenomatous polyposis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:07:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62029,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are presumed to arise from ovarian stromal cells, although the histogenesis is not firmly established. Mutually exclusive mutations in <em>CTNNB1</em> and <em>APC</em> result in aberrant nuclear immunoreactivity for &beta;-catenin and cyclin&nbsp;D1 through activation of the WNT/&beta;-catenin signalling pathway. Most tumours exhibit either a point mutation in <em>CTNNB1</em> (encoding &beta;-catenin) or, less frequently, mutation in <em>APC</em> {29076875; 26200099; 21881488; 25913412}. Mutations in <em>FOXL2</em> and <em>DICER1</em> have been absent in the tumours tested {27830327}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:07:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62030,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours are typically unilateral, with a mean size of about 9&nbsp;cm. The ovarian surface is smooth and on cut section usually predominantly solid, firm, and tan or tan-white, sometimes with cystic or haemorrhagic foci {18971779}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:07:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62031,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Most tumours exhibit a classic triad of microcysts, solid cellular zones, and fibrous stroma, which vary in proportion {18971779}. Microcysts are composed of small rounded to oval cystic spaces, in areas coalescing to larger irregular channels; intracytoplasmic vacuoles are also frequently present. The microcystic areas and solid cellular areas are well demarcated and intersected by fibrocollagenous stroma with hyaline plaques. Infrequently, tumours are focally, predominantly, or almost exclusively composed of diffuse, corded, and nested arrangements, with minimal or absent microcysts {30325056}. The cells contain a moderate amount of finely granular, lightly eosinophilic cytoplasm, with generally bland, round to oval or short spindle-shaped nuclei with small indistinct nucleoli. Bizarre nuclei with a symplastic appearance are sometimes present. Mitotic activity is low. The tumour cells are usually diffusely positive for &beta;-catenin (nuclear and cytoplasmic), CD10, WT1, FOXL2, cyclin&nbsp;D1, and SF1, while inhibin, calretinin, and EMA are typically negative {18971779; 26200099; 24684527}. Tumours may be positive for AR but are usually negative for ER and PR {30325056}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T17:57:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62032,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 72,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:07:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
